EXT. ICE CAVE UNDER A COLLAPSING GLACIER - DAY

Blue walls. Meltwater running in thin silver threads across the floor. Overhead, the ice groans like a living thing.

Two climbers in torn gear crouch near the cave mouth. Their ropes lie in a coil between them, one end frayed.

DR. LEOCADIA MWANGI-HALVORSEN (40s, glaciologist, split lip) tapes a gash in her thigh. She works fast, jaw clenched.

Across from her, her expedition partner TOBIAS ADEGOKE-LINDGREN (30s, mountaineer, frostbitten ears) tests the sharpness of an ice axe with his thumb.

LEOCADIA
Our rope won't hold another fall.

TOBIAS
Then we don't fall.

LEOCADIA
Tobias.

TOBIAS
Leocadia.

LEOCADIA
Eight hundred meters to the glacier terminus. Without a rope.

TOBIAS
Seven fifty.

LEOCADIA
Does it matter?

TOBIAS
It matters to me.

LEOCADIA
Why?

TOBIAS
Because it's less.

She laughs, then winces. He tosses her a chocolate bar. She catches it one-handed.

LEOCADIA
How many left?

TOBIAS
Three.

LEOCADIA
Four, if we split yours.

TOBIAS
I ate mine.

LEOCADIA
You always eat yours.

TOBIAS
I have a metabolism.

LEOCADIA
You have a problem.

Silence. The ice groans louder. A trickle of water becomes a stream, curling around their boots.

TOBIAS
Leo.

LEOCADIA
I hear it.

TOBIAS
The glacier's moving.

LEOCADIA
It's always moving.

TOBIAS
Faster.

She stands, wincing, and puts a hand against the wall. It hums.

LEOCADIA
We go. Now.

TOBIAS
Without gear?

LEOCADIA
Without everything.

They shoulder packs, scramble toward the cave mouth. The ceiling cracks, a long hairline racing across the blue. Tobias grabs her arm. She grabs his. They both look up as a slab the size of a truck shears loose.

They dive. The slab lands where they stood, spraying frozen shards. Leocadia tumbles through the opening, Tobias behind, both rolling into the white glare of day.

They lie on their backs, gasping. Behind them, the cave collapses, groaning, with a final thunderous sigh.

Dust settles. Snow glitters. A distant raven calls.

TOBIAS
(laughing, breathless)
Seven fifty.

LEOCADIA
Seven hundred. We just walked fifty.

TOBIAS
We crawled fifty.

LEOCADIA
Counts.

They sit up. She offers him the chocolate. He shakes his head. She breaks it in half, hands him the bigger piece.

TOBIAS
Leo.

LEOCADIA
Eat.

He eats. She watches the sky.

LEOCADIA (CONT'D)
(quietly)
I'm tired.

TOBIAS
I know.

LEOCADIA
Don't say anything.

TOBIAS
I won't.

They sit in the sun a long time. Neither speaks. The glacier moves under them like a slow breath.

Eventually she rises, offers him her good hand. He takes it. They start walking.
